#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Pre-commit gate for the Ohio budget corpus.
#
# Runs the checks that catch the failure this repository actually keeps having: a node
# asserting something about the repository that stopped being true. Those errors are cheap to
# fix at the moment of writing and expensive to find later, because both halves of the
# contradiction look correct on their own.
#
# Deliberately not the full `mise run ci`. Compiling and testing the workspace on every commit
# trains people to pass `--no-verify`, and a gate that gets bypassed protects nothing. Formatting,
# clippy, and the test suite stay in CI where they belong.
#
# Install with: mise run install-hooks
set -euo pipefail

root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
cd "$root"

# Only worth running if the commit touches something the corpus rules read.
if git diff --cached --name-only | grep -qE '^\.yidam/(corpus|catalog|decisions)/|^crates/corpus-'; then
  echo "pre-commit: validating the corpus"
  if ! mise run validate; then
    cat <<'MSG'

pre-commit: corpus validation failed.

If a claim went stale — a node saying a source is not catalogued when it is, or an
amount is unfilled when it holds a figure — fix the prose rather than the property.
Both halves look right in isolation, which is why this check exists.

To commit anyway: git commit --no-verify
MSG
    exit 1
  fi
fi
